import pf.Html
import ../../Demos/Birds
import ../../Demos/Snake
import ../../Demos/Timers
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/Note
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Demo

meta = {
	title: "Showcase",
	description: "Snake, a flock of bouncing Roc birds and the browser's timers: components Roc runs frame by frame in your browser, compiled to WebAssembly, with sound from a handler.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "snake", label: "Snake" },
		{ id: "flock", label: "A flock of Roc birds" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How they work" },
		{ id: "sound", label: "Sound, from a handler" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Showcase"
				title="A game and a flock, run by Roc"
			>
				Ordinary components, rendered by the server and hydrated in your browser, that also run on time.
				Each animation frame calls a Roc handler that moves things. Only the holes that read what moved
				run again, patching the few attributes that changed.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="snake">Snake</Heading>
			<p>
				Click the board, then steer with the arrows or WASD. Space pauses, M mutes, Tab leaves. Easy,
				Medium and Hard keep one speed; on Impossible, every fruit speeds the snake up.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="snake" idle="Roc is idle until you start: no frames run while the game waits.">
			<div data-demo="snake"><Snake seed={7} /></div>
		</Demo>

		<NoHydration>
			<Heading id="flock">A flock of Roc birds</Heading>
			<p>
				Type a number of birds, or use −5, −1, +1 and +5: new ones fly in, or the newest leave. Each
				bounces off the walls and the others, and squeaks when it bumps into one (once you've clicked the
				page: browsers keep pages quiet until then).
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="birds" idle="Measuring…">
			<div data-demo="birds"><Birds count={4} /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The line under each demo is measured live: a frame's time covers the handler that moves and
				collides the birds, the holes that read the flock, and their patches.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="how">How they work</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Time is a handler">
					<p>
						<code>on:frame.ms={"{|ms| send!(Frame(ms))}"}</code> runs its handler every animation frame,
						given the milliseconds since the last, while its element is on the page. The snake's sits
						inside an <code>if</code> that holds only while you play, so a paused game, like an empty sky,
						runs no frames.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Keys that do one thing">
					<p>
						<code>on:keydown.ArrowUp={"{|| send!(Turn(Up))}"}</code> runs for that key only and stops it
						scrolling the page. Other keys, Tab among them, work as usual.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Rows that stay, so little changes">
					<p>
						The birds are <code>.map</code> rows keyed by id, so each keeps its element; a move sets two
						custom properties the stylesheet turns into a translation. The snake's segments are rows by
						position (<code>{".map_with_index(|part, i| … key={i})"}</code>): each step, a segment takes
						the cell ahead, and a one-step CSS transition slides it there.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Pure, so it replays">
					<p>
						The rules are a plain function, <code>update</code>: last game and your move in, next game out,
						kept in a signal. Roc has no hidden randomness: the food's next place and a new bird's heading
						come from a seed in the game (<code>Demos/Random.roc</code>). The same moves give the same
						game, and every rule is an <code>expect</code> away from a test.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="What isn't shown isn't read">
					<p>
						The snake moves a cell per step's worth of frames. The time since its last move is a signal
						nothing reads, so a frame that only adds to it runs no hole and patches nothing.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<details>
				<summary>The snake: <code>{Sources.demo_snake.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={Sources.demo_snake.rocx} />
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>The flock: <code>{Sources.demo_birds.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_birds.file} source={Sources.demo_birds.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sound">Sound, from a handler</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>update</code> is pure, so it can't make a sound. The next game names its step's sound
				instead (<code>"eat"</code>, <code>"die"</code>, or nothing), which a test can check, and the
				handler that sets the game plays it with <code>Audio.play!</code>, from the
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>audio</a> plugin package (its script in the page plays it):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={"go! = |next| {\n\tset_game!(next)\n\tif (!next.muted and !next.sound.is_empty()) Audio.play!(\"/sounds/\${next.sound}.wav\") else {}\n}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				It plays any file the browser can (WAV, MP3, Ogg, from anywhere), with nothing prepared: the
				browser fetches each file once and keeps it. These are chiptune notes of a few KB, made by
				<code>site/sounds.mjs</code>. The flock plays one pop at every pitch with
				<code>{"Audio.play_with!(url, { volume, rate })"}</code>: each new bird plays it a little faster, a
				higher note.
			</p>
			<p>
				A sound plays once the handler is done and the page is patched. Only handlers play sounds; in a
				hole or on the server, it fails. For browser features with no platform module (the clipboard,
				say), a handler calls the page's JavaScript with <code>{"Browser.call!(name, arg)"}</code>, like
				Elm's ports. Nothing comes back; for answers, ask the server
				(<a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a>).
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Watch it happen">
				<p>
					Inspect a flying bird: only its <code>style</code> changes, frame after frame, in the
					hundredths Roc rounds to.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				For “every tenth of a second” or “once the typing stops”, use the browser's timers, from
				<code>pf.Solid</code>: <code>set_interval!(ms, tick!)</code> and <code>set_timeout!(ms, done!)</code>
				run a handler later, as a click would, and return a <code>clear!</code> function that stops it.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game>
			<div data-demo="timers"><Timers /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				The stopwatch keeps its interval's <code>clear!</code> in a signal, and Stop calls it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={"(watch!, set_watch!) = create_signal!(stopped)\ntoggle! = ||\n\tmatch watch!() {\n\t\tRunning(clear!) => {\n\t\t\tclear!()\n\t\t\tset_watch!(Stopped)\n\t\t}\n\t\tStopped => set_watch!(Running(set_interval!(100, || set_tenths!(tenths!() + 1))))\n\t}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				On each key, the search clears the last timeout and starts its own, so it searches once, 400 ms
				after the last key. The clock clears nothing: a timer belongs to what started it (a component, or
				the part of the page a handler's element is in) and stops when that goes. Hide the clock and its
				ticks stop. Solid needs <code>{"onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id))"}</code> for this; here it is
				built in.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="The server starts none">
				<p>
					No timer runs on the server: the clock renders at 0, and the browser starts its interval as it
					hydrates.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<details>
				<summary>The timers, and the clock: <code>{Sources.demo_timers.file}</code>, <code>{Sources.demo_clock.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={Sources.demo_timers.rocx} />
				<Code title={Sources.demo_clock.file} source={Sources.demo_clock.rocx} />
			</details>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
